    "turn_text": "for the president right away, like say that afternoon or the very next day after a tragedy such as, you know, Hurricane Michael or tornado area. So at the very least, do a slow flyover in Air Force One to survey the damage, not to just get pictures, but to see it with his own eyes. And granted, you won't really be able to see it that great. But the optics of that, when you are on the ground, And your entire world has been turned upside down. And you see Air Force One fly over, at least slow enough to see that the president, and I don't even care if the president is even in the plane. I mean, you can do, and not that it should go that way, but if you want to go full on optics on this, have Air Force One fly over the entire area. So people see it so they can at least go, all right, so the president's on Air Force One, he came through and he looked. Because granted, the president's gonna get satellite photos and drone photos and all this other stuff, so he'll be able to see it. But I'm not criticizing him the fact that it did take him a little while to get in there. Because I would have criticized him more if he went in right away. Because I think search and rescue right away is the priority, not bringing in the president, regardless of who the president is. I'd say the same thing about Donald Trump. But when he did finally get over to Kentucky yesterday, He was not greeted with what you would think he would be greeted. Instead, he was greeted with the let's go Brandon Chance as he arrived to view the damage that was caused by the tornadoes. That says a lot. I mean my entire world has just been turned upside down, my town has been wiped out, probably lost my house, my neighbors lost their house, I lost friends, I lost neighbors. And the first thing that I want to do when the President of the United States comes in is give him the let's go Brandon chant, which we all know what let's go Brandon really truly means. And earlier in the day Biden had spoken a briefing from local leaders at Kentucky about the storm and then he pulled, of course, his coronavirus mask under his chin to speak, reminding leaders of the importance to come together to help the community rebuild. And he said that there's no red tornadoes or blue tornadoes. There's no red states or blue states when this stuff happens. And that's true. We all need to come together, put politics aside. But the fact that they were more more concerned about saying, let's go Brandon, than they were the president coming in to view the damage says a lot about the president. It really does. And of course, Jill Biden, she finally got out, and she went out to visit some of the children that were injured in the Waukesau Christmas Parade massacre. Not the car accident, the massacre. Let's call it right. And she visited several of the wounded kids from that Christmas parade yesterday. And then she turned around and also used this occasion to remind parents to vaccinate their children for coronavirus. Furiously. Not the time in place for that jilly girl. Oh my God, you know, the children got, you know, hit and killed because of this maniac that came through and drove the car. By the way, did you know that you can protect your children a little bit better if you'd get them vaccinated? I mean, is this like another moment of, you know, like, hey, there's a hurricane coming and you need to get vaccinated because you can't survive a hurricane unless you're vaccinated. You remember when Brandon said that one? I mean, all the other times that we've been able to survive hurricanes and tropical storms and everything, it didn't get vaccinated. We survived, but this time, oh, if you don't get vaccinated, you're not going to be able to survive that hurricane. But I think it was very poor taste for the first lady to jump out there and go out to visit these injured children and parents who are probably still rather distraught by the fact that this maniac drove through the Christmas parade and killed some of their neighbors and their friends and ran over their children. Do you really think the last thing that they want to hear is Jill Biden, the vice, the president's wife, the first lady coming in and saying, Just remember, you got to get your children vaccinated. Get those kids vaccinated so that they'll be okay. During her remarks, when she spoke to some young parents feeling absolute terror when their children were born, because you know that it's a little fragile life is depending on you. And she said, we absolutely do everything we can to protect our kids and shifting the discussion over to the coronavirus. I mean, poor taste, absolutely poor taste for that to happen. But then again, should we expect anything less from the White House? I mean, seriously. We got to take a break coming up here in just second for Fox news, a little bit of traffic updates for you too. There is an accident over in Michigan Avenue in 25th street. Apparently it is one with injuries, but it's not blocking things, but do be careful going through there also still the accident over at Wildwood on front beach road. So use caution there when you're coming through. That was another accident reported with injuries. And of course, if you are also transmitter in 231, they are still working on the railroad tracks up in that area. So it may cause a few delays right there in that area. So watch out for some of that traffic, OK? Hang on. We will be right back. This is the Brian Rush show. You're listening to News Talk at 101. Good morning.",
